<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ain</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ain department</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ain is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, in the Auvergne-Rhône-Alpes region, named after the river Ain, a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a>. It lies between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Saône</a> and the Jura mountains and borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, with its prefecture at Bourg-en-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bresse">Bresse</a>. In ancient times part of it was inhabited by the Ambarri, a Gallic people allied to the Aedui.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ain.jpg" alt="Ain" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benoît Prieur · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monast%C3%A8re_royal_de_Brou_(%C3%A9glise)_(1).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
